1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,002
This is MuleTrace, a risk,

2
00:00:01,964 --> 00:00:06,065
fraud and regulatory intelligence copilot for banks and NBFCs.

3
00:00:06,679 --> 00:00:10,178
It finds mule-account networks hidden in transaction data,

4
00:00:10,184 --> 00:00:12,912
explains each one with evidence and policy,

5
00:00:12,874 --> 00:00:16,137
and drafts an audit-ready suspicious transaction report.

6
00:00:16,690 --> 00:00:18,068
Here is the problem.

7
00:00:18,705 --> 00:00:22,582
Ten lakh rupees can leave a bank as two thousand transfers of five

8
00:00:22,444 --> 00:00:24,870
hundred rupees, through a hundred mule accounts.

9
00:00:25,490 --> 00:00:29,513
Every transfer sits below every threshold, so no rule fires.

10
00:00:30,122 --> 00:00:32,571
The fraud is only visible as a network.

11
00:00:33,120 --> 00:00:34,676
Let's run it end to end.

12
00:00:35,291 --> 00:00:38,019
The input is a synthetic month of banking data:

13
00:00:38,036 --> 00:00:42,149
ten thousand transactions, six hundred accounts with KYC details,

14
00:00:42,110 --> 00:00:44,291
and our AML policy documents.

15
00:00:44,911 --> 00:00:45,776
Processing:

16
00:00:45,804 --> 00:00:49,325
the graph detector scans every transaction for four laundering

17
00:00:49,186 --> 00:00:50,173
typologies.

18
00:00:50,793 --> 00:00:54,358
The output is six suspicious networks, ranked by risk.

19
00:00:54,978 --> 00:00:57,852
Because the data carries hidden ground-truth labels,

20
00:00:57,813 --> 00:00:59,247
we can score ourselves:

21
00:00:59,275 --> 00:01:03,298
100% precision and 99.95%

22
00:01:03,159 --> 00:01:05,474
recall, in about a tenth of a second.

23
00:01:06,020 --> 00:01:09,474
This is the investigator workspace, live on Cloudflare.

24
00:01:10,088 --> 00:01:13,821
The header shows the month at a glance: transactions, accounts,

25
00:01:13,782 --> 00:01:16,790
networks found, and thirty lakh rupees at risk.

26
00:01:17,410 --> 00:01:20,283
On the left is the alert queue, ranked by risk,

27
00:01:20,245 --> 00:01:21,968
with a filter by typology.

28
00:01:22,588 --> 00:01:26,645
The canvas draws every network the detector found: a structuring ring,

29
00:01:26,617 --> 00:01:30,629
two layering chains, a fan-out and fan-in, and two round-trip cycles.

30
00:01:31,180 --> 00:01:34,276
Let's open the top alert, F001.

31
00:01:34,902 --> 00:01:38,680
A hundred accounts sent two thousand small transfers to one collector,

32
00:01:38,708 --> 00:01:41,805
eighty-four percent of them exactly five hundred rupees.

33
00:01:42,447 --> 00:01:46,392
The risk score of ninety-eight is fully explainable: pattern detected,

34
00:01:46,398 --> 00:01:50,275
amount at stake, account red flags, and pattern strength.

35
00:01:50,896 --> 00:01:56,380
Below it is the exact clause the network breaches, AML-04 section 3.2,

36
00:01:56,342 --> 00:01:58,133
quoted with its source document,

37
00:01:58,150 --> 00:02:01,191
followed by every account and every transaction,

38
00:02:01,208 --> 00:02:04,048
exportable as a CSV evidence pack.

39
00:02:04,600 --> 00:02:06,870
Clicking an account opens its profile.

40
00:02:07,485 --> 00:02:11,229
This collector was opened just thirty-one days before the activity,

41
00:02:11,202 --> 00:02:12,736
holds minimum KYC,

42
00:02:12,697 --> 00:02:16,151
and received sixty-three times its declared monthly income.

43
00:02:16,782 --> 00:02:18,550
Its counterparties are listed,

44
00:02:18,546 --> 00:02:20,716
and the graph highlights its neighbourhood.

45
00:02:21,336 --> 00:02:22,702
Names stay masked,

46
00:02:22,697 --> 00:02:26,341
because an investigator doesn't need personal data to triage.

47
00:02:26,890 --> 00:02:29,886
The same evidence exists for every typology.

48
00:02:30,501 --> 00:02:33,184
F005 is a fan-out and fan-in:

49
00:02:33,146 --> 00:02:35,796
one source splits money across thirty mules,

50
00:02:35,802 --> 00:02:37,916
just under ten thousand rupees each,

51
00:02:37,889 --> 00:02:41,521
and they pass ninety-nine percent of it to one collector within hours.

52
00:02:42,153 --> 00:02:44,434
F002 is a layering chain:

53
00:02:44,485 --> 00:02:48,508
seven and a half lakh rupees hop through six accounts in twelve hours.

54
00:02:49,150 --> 00:02:51,488
F003 is a round trip:

55
00:02:51,550 --> 00:02:54,512
money loops through four accounts and comes back home.

56
00:02:55,060 --> 00:02:56,293
Now the copilot.

57
00:02:56,918 --> 00:02:58,385
Ask in plain English:

58
00:02:58,369 --> 00:03:02,180
which accounts received over five lakh rupees in transfers under a

59
00:03:02,041 --> 00:03:02,772
thousand?

60
00:03:03,392 --> 00:03:05,885
It finds the collector, links the finding,

61
00:03:05,858 --> 00:03:07,682
and quotes the policy clause.

62
00:03:08,302 --> 00:03:09,825
Ask it to trace money,

63
00:03:09,809 --> 00:03:13,307
and it follows the funds from the fan-out source through thirty mules

64
00:03:13,168 --> 00:03:15,394
to one account within forty-eight hours.

65
00:03:16,014 --> 00:03:19,847
Ask a regulatory question, and it answers from the policy text:

66
00:03:19,887 --> 00:03:23,385
an STR is due to FIU-IND within seven working days.

67
00:03:24,006 --> 00:03:26,075
Every answer lists its sources.

68
00:03:26,630 --> 00:03:29,336
Every alert ends in a documented decision.

69
00:03:29,951 --> 00:03:33,851
The investigator adds a note and escalates to the Principal Officer.

70
00:03:34,471 --> 00:03:37,746
Switching the role to Principal Officer unmasks names,

71
00:03:37,830 --> 00:03:40,324
just as a Snowflake masking policy would.

72
00:03:40,944 --> 00:03:44,844
One click drafts the suspicious transaction report: subjects,

73
00:03:44,794 --> 00:03:49,464
transactions, grounds of suspicion, policy basis, recommended actions,

74
00:03:49,459 --> 00:03:50,893
and the filing deadline.

75
00:03:51,513 --> 00:03:54,141
Only the Principal Officer can mark it filed,

76
00:03:54,102 --> 00:03:56,496
and every step lands in the audit log.

77
00:03:57,050 --> 00:04:00,280
Under the hood, MuleTrace is designed Snowflake-native.

78
00:04:00,895 --> 00:04:04,137
Transactions and policy documents land in Snowflake.

79
00:04:04,757 --> 00:04:07,474
Dynamic Tables keep flow features fresh.

80
00:04:08,094 --> 00:04:11,458
The same Python detector runs as a Snowpark procedure.

81
00:04:12,089 --> 00:04:14,304
Cortex Search indexes the policies,

82
00:04:14,288 --> 00:04:17,608
and a Cortex Agent combines a semantic view with search,

83
00:04:17,636 --> 00:04:21,000
so every answer carries both numbers and citations.

84
00:04:21,550 --> 00:04:25,450
The workflow is packaged as three modular CoCo CLI skills:

85
00:04:25,445 --> 00:04:29,881
ingest for input, detect for processing, and investigate for output.

86
00:04:30,507 --> 00:04:33,759
Each is a reusable skill, versioned in the repository.

87
00:04:34,310 --> 00:04:37,038
The results: every planted scheme caught,

88
00:04:37,033 --> 00:04:40,844
no legitimate look-alikes flagged, and a complete path from signal,

89
00:04:40,839 --> 00:04:42,909
to evidence, to a filed report.

90
00:04:43,460 --> 00:04:44,447
MuleTrace.

91
00:04:45,062 --> 00:04:47,678
Follow the money, and file with confidence.
